// Case (b) is not implied by "no loop write writes a value derived from a
// read of the same iteration".
//
// The loop's only write stores the constant 1, and its only read feeds nothing
// but the guard, so the hypothesis holds. Thread 2 copies x into y. In an
// execution where the second iteration reads y from thread 2, its value comes
// along
//
//   W x:=1 (iter 1) -rf-> R x (T2) -DP(data)-> W y:=rt (T2) -rf-> R y (iter 2)
//
// so an earlier iteration reaches the write w = (W y) in (DP u rf)+ and case
// (b) fails. Cases (a) and (c) do not apply: w is neither in the iteration nor
// before the loop, and is not a read-don't-modify-write.
//
// The acquire read, release write and SC fence are there only so that
// Condition 4 holds and case (b) is the one condition at stake. Release and
// acquire alone do not order an iteration's write before the next
// iteration's read, at another location; the fence does. Without it MoRDor
// reports Condition 4 failing as well.
//
// MoRDor (63403b7) reports the loop episodic, conditions 1-4 all satisfied: its
// Condition 2 compares a read only against writes of the same loop and does
// not follow (DP u rf)+ through other threads. caseb_path.py over the
// `dependencies --output-mode json` export finds the path in 2/18 executions
// at bound 2 and 12/38 at bound 4.
rX := malloc 1;
*rX := 0;
rY := malloc 1;
*rY := 0;
{
  do
  {
    ry :acq= *rY;
    *rX :rel= 1;
    fence(sc);
  }
  while (ry = 0)
}
|||
{
  rt := *rX;
  *rY := rt;
}
